% ============================================================================
% solid-vertices-edges — how many vertices and edges each Platonic solid has
% (adj-facts-stdlib, GEOMETRY).
% ============================================================================
% The companion of solid-shapes.adj (solid -> FACES): this ships the other two
% columns of the same MathWorld "Platonic Solid" table — solid -> VERTICES and
% solid -> EDGES. Together the three libraries carry the full (V, E, F) triple
% for each of the five Platonic solids, so a consumer can recall all three and
% CHECK Euler's formula for convex polyhedra, V - E + F = 2, entirely on the CPU:
%
%     ? solid_vertices(cube, $V)       % 8
%     ? solid_edges(cube, $E)          % 12
%     % with solid_faces(cube, 6) from solid-shapes.adj: 8 - 12 + 6 = 2. ✓
%
% Each is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): every `row` lowers to a ground
% relation carrying the table's citation, so the lookup is the ordinary SLD
% binding query — the engine returns the count WITH its source, on the CPU, with
% zero model calls, and abstains on a solid not in the table. Because the values
% are NUMBERS, a recalled count composes straight into a formula — the same
% concrete RECALL -> COMPUTE bridge as its faces sibling.
%
% Provenance — both `source` fields are SENTENCES FROM THE PAGE, and until
% installment 4h neither was. Each held author-composed prose ABOUT the page
% instead: "MathWorld's Platonic Solid table gives, for each solid, its
% number of vertices: tetrahedron 4, cube 8, ...". Accurate, and not a
% quotation of anything — it names the source in the third person, which no
% verbatim span of a page ever does. A field whose contract is a contiguous
% span of its `locator` held a summary, and no reader could tell without
% fetching.
%
% The counts did not need the page's TABLE, which is why this repair decides
% none of the questions held on #14111. MathWorld states them in ordinary
% prose, and the `source` of each table below is now that prose, verbatim
% and contiguous.
%
% WHAT THE VERTICES SENTENCE GROUNDS, exactly. It gives the numbers in FACE
% order — "4, 6, 8, 12, 20 ... in the order tetrahedron, cube, octahedron,
% dodecahedron, icosahedron" — and then states the VERTEX order separately:
% "which is also the ordered number of vertices (in the order tetrahedron,
% octahedron, cube, icosahedron, dodecahedron)". The five rows are those two
% read together, so each row is an INFERENCE the sentence licenses outright,
% NOT a per-row verbatim claim. Every row below marks which position it
% takes. The edges sentence needs no such care: it states its own order
% inline, with `=` marking the two pairs that share a count.
%
% A CLAIM THIS FILE DOES NOT MAKE. Installment 4g's five repairs are
% byte-exact against RAW HTML. These two are not, and cannot be: the page's
% own bytes carry five newlines inside the vertices sentence, because the
% HTML source wraps the paragraph. They are verbatim under the extractor's
% whitespace collapse — the same standard every one of this stdlib's
% currently-verbatim values already meets — and that is a WEAKER claim than
% 4g's. The two look alike and are not; whether `source` means document text
% or the page as rendered is filed on #14111 and is not decided here.
%
% `trust authoritative` — a primary mathematics reference. Nothing here is
% asserted from memory. (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

% ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Vertices — the corner points where edges meet.
% ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
table solid_vertices {
    columns solid, vertices

    row (tetrahedron, 4)      % 1st of the vertex order, 1st number
    row (cube, 8)             % 3rd of the vertex order, 3rd number
    row (octahedron, 6)       % 2nd of the vertex order, 2nd number
    row (dodecahedron, 20)    % 5th of the vertex order, 5th number
    row (icosahedron, 12)     % 4th of the vertex order, 4th number

    source "The ordered number of faces for the Platonic solids are 4, 6, 8, 12, 20 (OEIS A053016; in the order tetrahedron, cube, octahedron, dodecahedron, icosahedron), which is also the ordered number of vertices (in the order tetrahedron, octahedron, cube, icosahedron, dodecahedron)."
    locator "https://mathworld.wolfram.com/PlatonicSolid.html"
    trust authoritative
}

% ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Edges — the line segments where two faces meet.
% ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
table solid_edges {
    columns solid, edges

    row (tetrahedron, 6)      % 1st in the sentence's own order
    row (cube, 12)            % "octahedron = cube", 2nd count
    row (octahedron, 12)      % "octahedron = cube", 2nd count
    row (dodecahedron, 30)    % "dodecahedron = icosahedron", 3rd count
    row (icosahedron, 30)     % "dodecahedron = icosahedron", 3rd count

    source "The ordered number of edges are 6, 12, 12, 30, 30 (OEIS A063722; in the order tetrahedron, octahedron = cube, dodecahedron = icosahedron)."
    locator "https://mathworld.wolfram.com/PlatonicSolid.html"
    trust authoritative
}
